Known limitations
=================

Moonrise / moonset accuracy near new moon
-----------------------------------------

The moonrises that bound the lunar days are computed with the three-point
interpolation algorithm from Meeus, *Astronomical Algorithms*, chapter 15
(rise.ApproxTimes seeding a convergence loop in moonrise()).

On most days the values agree with published almanacs (for example
drikpanchang.com) to within 1 minute. However, within roughly ±1 day of the
new moon the Moon's right ascension changes fast enough that the three-point
interpolation becomes unreliable: the reported moonrise can drift by up to
40 minutes, and the loop can converge on a rise moment belonging to an
adjacent date rather than the target one.

Observed example (Kolkata, 22.5333°N, 88.3667°E, April 2026):

    Apr 1, 2, 9, 10   agreement within 1 minute
    Apr 16, 17        moonrise off by 30-40 minutes (new moon on Apr 17);
                      e.g. Apr 17 rise reported 04:06 IST vs. 04:46 almanac

Root cause: the three-point Lagrange interpolation of the Moon's α/δ over a
±1-day window (interp.NewLen3) is too coarse when the Moon's motion is
changing fastest, near syzygy, so the horizon-crossing solution can land on
the wrong day.

Workarounds for users who need sub-minute accuracy near new moon:

  - Cross-check against an external almanac for those dates.
  - Replace rise.Times with a direct altitude root finder that samples
    moonposition.Position at fine time steps (e.g. 1 minute) and bisects
    across the horizon crossing.
  - Use a higher precision ephemeris (e.g. JPL DE440 via SPICE).

The tithi start/end times themselves are not affected by this limitation;
only the moonrise and moonset lines printed alongside each tithi.
